artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a bounded domain occupied by an elastic composite with $\varepsilon$-periodic microstructure. Inside the reference periodicity cell $Y = (0,1)^3$, suppose there is a smooth (possibly curved, not necessarily planar) fissure $\Gamma \subset Y$ — i.e., an oriented surface with two lips $\Gamma^\pm$ — and let $\Gamma_\varepsilon \subset \Omega$ denote the $\varepsilon$-periodic family of such fissures. The bulk material satisfies linear elasticity with periodic Hooke tensor $A(y)$, and on $\Gamma_\varepsilon$ the two lips interact through unilateral contact and Coulomb friction: the normal jump satisfies the Signorini conditions (no interpenetration, non-positive contact pressure, complementarity), and the tangential traction $\sigma_T$ and tangential jump $[u]_T$ obey Coulomb's law $|\sigma_T| \le \mu |\sigma_N|$ with sliding only when equality holds and then $\dot{[u]}_T$ aligned opposite to $\sigma_T$, where $\mu > 0$ is the friction coefficient. The composite is loaded quasi-statically by a prescribed history of macroscopic strain $E(t)$.

The problem: Derive a homogenized (macroscopic) constitutive description of this composite that is valid without the restriction that the fissure is a straight segment in a two-dimensional setting and without the restriction that the fissure is either fully open or fully closed throughout the loading history. Specifically, identify a (possibly multivalued) macroscopic constitutive map together with internal/hidden sliding variables $\alpha(t)$ that describe relative lip motion, such that:

(i) for each history $t \mapsto E(t)$ and each admissible history $t \mapsto \alpha(t)$, one obtains a local periodic corrector field $w(t, y)$ on $Y \setminus \Gamma$ satisfying the cell-level equilibrium, contact, and friction conditions; 

(ii) the macroscopic Cauchy stress $\Sigma(t)$ is recovered as the $Y$-average of the microscopic stress $A(y)(E(t) + e_y w(t, y))$; 

(iii) the framework correctly accounts for the intrinsic non-uniqueness of quasi-static frictional contact (rate-independence, possible bifurcations of sliding regime, partial opening/closing of the fissure).

Prove existence of such a homogenized formulation, characterize its mathematical structure (e.g. as a rate-independent system with hysteresis), and establish a rigorous two-scale or unfolding convergence theorem linking the $\varepsilon$-problems to the homogenized model.
